    <item>
      <title>A Quick Overview on Memory Allocators</title>
      <link>https://sameepkathayat.com.np/a-quick-overview-on-memory-allocators/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 21 Jul 2026 07:28:26 +0545</pubDate>
      <guid>https://sameepkathayat.com.np/a-quick-overview-on-memory-allocators/</guid>
      <description>&lt;img src=&#34;https://sameepkathayat.com.np/images/memory.webp&#34; /&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;This article mainly focuses on three popular general purpose allocators: glibc&amp;#39;s &lt;code class=&#34;verbatim&#34;&gt;ptmalloc2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code class=&#34;verbatim&#34;&gt;jemalloc&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code class=&#34;verbatim&#34;&gt;tcmalloc&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;headline-1&#34;&gt;&#xA;Understanding Memory Management: Stack vs. Heap&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Memory management is a fundamental aspect of programming, influencing performance, security, and reliability. Broadly, there are two primary strategies: &lt;strong&gt;stack-based&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;heap-based&lt;/strong&gt; memory allocation. Each has distinct characteristics, trade-offs, and use cases.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
